"""Versioned prompts for the researched episode workflow."""

PROMPT_VERSION = "episode-flow-1"

STYLE = """Write an original, prepared academic science discussion for a general audience, with four fictional voices: a curious, well-prepared host and three complementary specialists with distinct expertise and speaking rhythms. Never imitate Melvyn Bragg or any BBC programme, broadcaster, trademark, or signature presentation. The host guides the exchange with directed questions and useful follow-ups; specialists explain evidence, history, concrete examples, limits, and live uncertainties. Let voices interject naturally and disagree thoughtfully when the evidence warrants it. Begin with a compact, inviting setup and introductions that take about one minute before the first substantive question. Keep warmth restrained, with occasional dry humor rather than gags. Make the science understandable to a secondary-school student without flattening nuance. Close clearly and quietly by returning to the central question. Stay within the bounded topic, ground factual assertions in the dossier, and never invent citations or imply that a specialist was interviewed. Length options are 15, 30, or 45 minutes."""

PROMPTS = {
    "outline": "Create a compact episode outline for {minutes} minutes on: {topic}. Bound the scope, define the central question, opening minute, segment durations, voices/expertise, key uncertainties, and conclusion. Segment duration_minutes MUST add exactly to {minutes}. Plan about 130 spoken words per minute (total about {total_words} words); each segment includes id, title, duration_minutes, and word_budget equal to duration*130. Return JSON object with title, scope, central_question, segments (array), voices (array of 4), uncertainties (array), word_budget.",
    "research": "Build a concise source dossier for this outline: {outline}. Use web search. Prefer primary research, reviews, and authoritative institutions. Include only sources you actually inspected; provide title, URL, publication date if available, locator or relevant finding, and limits. Map each factual finding to source IDs. Return JSON object with sources and findings; distinguish evidence from interpretation.",
    "draft": "Write only this episode segment using the outline and dossier. {style} Target {minutes} minutes total. This batch covers: {sections}. Stay within 10% of its word budget. Keep continuity with prior section summary: {continuity}. Ground every factual turn in one or more dossier finding IDs in claim_ids; the findings themselves cite source IDs. Do not make unsupported factual assertions. Framing and thanks can use an empty claim_ids array. Include a short opening minute in segment 1. Return a JSON object with turns (id, speaker keys host, expert1, expert2, expert3; text; claim_ids; delivery) and section_summary. Dossier: {dossier}",
    "review": "Audit this draft against the source dossier. Check each factual claim and citation, flag unsupported, overstated, outdated, or ambiguous statements, and propose only targeted replacement edits. Do not rewrite unaffected prose. Return JSON object with findings (turn_id, severity, issue, source_ids, minimal_replacement), unresolved_questions, and approved_claims. Draft: {draft}. Dossier: {dossier}",
    "speech": "Convert the reviewed draft into a speech-ready script and asset plan. Preserve factual wording and four speaker roles; remove source IDs from spoken text, retain turn IDs, add natural pauses/section transitions, and mark pronunciation notes separately. Provide per-turn audio asset records with speaker, text, delivery, estimated seconds, and voice requirements. Do not claim to generate audio. Draft: {draft}. Review: {review}",
}
